{
  "event_contract_version": "skill-events-v2",
  "known_answer_source": "Expected values are hand-authored, never generated from parser output. Transcript rows carry only the key topology a content-free diagnostic observed on a real host: tool identity, tool_use id, the invoked skill name, and result linkage. Tool arguments, briefs, prompts, project names and result bodies are sanitized out.",
  "contract_boundary": "These fixtures pin the transcript-to-invocation projection only. A matching projection proves an extractor agrees on shape and ordering; it never proves a skill was loaded on a live host, and invocation counts are a manipulation check, never an effectiveness claim.",
  "registered_drivers": ["claude-code", "opencode"],
  "driver_notes": {
    "claude-code": "A dedicated Skill tool. One invocation spans two events: a tool_use block carrying the skill identity, and a separate tool_result block linked by tool_use_id. Skill identities are namespaced (bundle:name).",
    "opencode": "A dedicated skill tool. One invocation is a single record holding the call and its outcome together: the invoked name, an opaque call id, and a terminal status, so no call/result pairing is needed. Skill identities are bare names with no bundle prefix.",
    "open_decisions": [
      "Identifier normalization: the same CCL skill appears namespaced on one driver and bare on another, so a cross-driver comparison needs an agreed normal form before invocation identity can be compared across drivers.",
      "tool_use_id uniqueness scope: this contract requires ids unique across all skill invocations, but a real driver emits short literal ids that repeat across sessions. Either the scope narrows to one session, or literal-id invocations stay unverifiable. Until that is decided, the boundary case below pins the current global rule."
    ]
  },
  "unregistered_driver_semantics": "A driver whose transcript shape is not registered yields invocation_unverifiable for every run: the item records the degradation, keeps outcome evidence usable, and never converts missing invocation data into a compliance pass. Unregistered means no known-answer case has been authored and agreed for that shape; it does not assert that the driver emits no observable invocation evidence.",
  "cases": [
